COMMAND NAME: gpmfr

Manages the Greenplum Database backup images that are stored on a 
primary Data Domain system and a remote Data Domain system that is used 
for disaster recovery. Managed file replication is used for disaster 
recovery by the Data Domain Boost software option to transfer a backup 
image from one Data Domain system to another. 


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpmfr --delete {OLDEST | <timestamp>} [--remote] 
[--master-port=<master_port>] [--skip-ping] 

gpmfr {--replicate | --restore} {LATEST | <timestamp>} 
--max-streams <max_IO_streams> [--master-port=<master_port>] 
[--verbose] [--skip-ping]

gpmfr {--list | --list-files <timestamp>} [--remote]
[--master-port=<master_port>] [--skip-ping] 

gpmfr --show-streams [--remote] [--master-port=<master_port>] 
[--skip-ping]

gpmfr -h | --help

gpmfr --version


*****************************************************
PREREQUISITES
*****************************************************

The Data Domain systems that are used as primary and remote backup 
systems for managed file replication must have Data Domain Boost 
enabled. 

The Greenplum Database master segment must be able to connect to both 
primary Data Domain system and the remote Data Domain system. 


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************
 The gpmfr utility to provides these capabilities: 

* Lists the backup data sets that are on the primary or the remote
  Data Domain system. 

* Replicates a backup data set that is on the primary Data Domain
  system to the remote system. 

* Recovers a backup data set that is on the remote Data Domain system
  to the primary system. 

* Deletes a backup data set that is on the primary or the remote 
  Data Domain system.

The Greenplum Database backup sets are identified by timestamps 
(yyyymmddhhmmss). 

gpmfr attempts to schedule the replication task for the files in backup 
data set, if no I/O stream is available, gpmfr retries the replication 
until an I/O stream is available. 

When cancelling a replication operation, gpmfr kills all active 
replication processes and cleans up all the files on replication Data 
Domain system. 

Greenplum Database supports Data Domain Boost SDK version 2.4.1 with 
DDOS 5.0.1.0, 5.1 and 5.2. 


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

--delete OLDEST

 Deletes the oldest backup set (first in chronological order) from the 
 primary Data Domain system. 

 Specify the option --remote to delete the backup set from the remote 
 Data Domain system.


--delete <timestamp> 

 Deletes the Greenplum Database backup set identified by the <timestamp>
 from the primary Data Domain system. 
 
 Specify the option --remote to delete the backup set from the remote 
 Data Domain system. 


--list

 Lists the Greenplum Database backup sets that are on the primary Data 
 Domain system. The backup sets are identified by timestamps 
 (yyyymmddhhmmss). 
 
 Specify the option --remote to list the Greenplum Database backup sets 
 that are on the remote Data Domain system. 


--list-files <timestamp>

 Lists the files in the Greenplum Database backup set specified by the 
 <timestamp> that is on the primary Data Domain system. 

 Specify the option --remote to list the files in the backup set that is
 on the remote Data Domain system. 


--master-port=<master_port>

 Specify the Greenplum Database master port number. The utility retrieves 
 information from the Greenplum Database instance that uses the port 
 number to validate backup sets. If the option is not specified, the 
 default value is 5432. 

 If gpmfr does not find a Greenplum Database, validation is skipped and a 
 warning is displayed. 

--max-streams <max_IO_streams>

 Specifies the maximum number of Data Domain I/O streams that can be used
 when copying the backup set between the primary and remote Data Domain 
 systems.


--replicate LATEST

 Replicates the most recent available backup set that is on the primary 
 Data Domain system to the remote system. 

 If a backup set with the same timestamp exists on remote Data Domain 
 system, the utility prompts you to confirm replacing the backup. 

 A progress bar indicating transfer status of the backup set is shown at 
 the output. 

 Note: A backup set must be completely backed up to primary Data Domain 
 system before it can be replicated to the remote Data Domain system. 


--replicate <timestamp>

 Replicates the Greenplum Database backup set that is specified by the 
 <timestamp> from the primary Data Domain system to the remote system. 

 If a backup set with the same <timestamp> exists on remote Data Domain 
 system, the utility prompts you to confirm replacing the backup.

 A progress bar indicating transfer status of the backup set is shown at 
 the output. 

 Note: The backup set identified by <timestamp> must be completely backed 
 up to primary Data Domain system before it can be replicated to the 
 remote Data Domain system. 

--remote 

 Perform the operation on the remote Data Domain system that is used for 
 disaster recovery. 

 For example, gpmfr --list lists the backup sets that are on the primary 
 Data Domain system that is use to back up Greenplum Database. 
 gpmfr --list --remote lists the backup sets that are on the remote system. 
 

--restore LATEST

 Restores the latest Greenplum Database backup set that is available on 
 the remote Data Domain system to the primary system. 

 If a backup set with the same <timestamp> exists on primary Data Domain 
 system, the utility prompts you to confirm replacing the backup.

 A progress bar indicating transfer status of the backup set is shown on 
 shown at the output. 


--restore <timestamp>

 Restores the Greenplum Database backup set that is identified by the 
 <timestamp> from the remote Data Domain system to the primary system. 

 If a backup set with the same <timestamp> exists on primary Data Domain 
 system, the utility prompts you to confirm replacing the backup. 
 
 A progress bar indicating transfer status of the backup set is shown on 
 shown at the output. 


--show-streams 

 Displays the replication I/O stream soft limit and the number of I/O 
 streams that are in use. 

 
--skip-ping 

 Before performing an operation, ping is used to ensure that the Data 
 Domain system is reachable. If the Data Domain host is configured to 
 block ICMP ping probes, specify this option to skip the ping of the Data 
 Domain system. 


--verbose 

 When replicating or restoring a backup set, a progress bar for each file 
 in the backup set is shown. An estimated duration is displayed with each 
 progress bar. The estimate is based on the current rate of transfer. 

 When you run gpmfr with this option, the transfer status for each file 
 in the backup set is periodically written to a log file in the 
 gpAdminLogs directory. A directory is created with the with name 
 gpmfr_timestamp. In the gpmfr_timestamp directory, a log file 
 backupfilename_transfer.log is written for each backup file 
 
 
-h | --help

 Displays the online help. 


--version 

 Displays the version of this utility. 


*****************************************************
EXAMPLE
*****************************************************

The following example replicates the latest backup set on the primary 
Data Domain sever to the remote server. During the replication, a 
progress bar for each file in the backup set is shown. 

-replicate LATEST --verbose


